            1.2. Theoretical framework (about 5 lines)

 Multi-sensory discomfort arises from complex environmental exposures, yet most studies
 focus on stationary subjects (1, 2). This overlooks the dynamic interaction between
 environment, perception, and stress in mobile users (3, 4, 5, 6). Thermal acclimatization—
 how quickly the body adjusts between shaded parks, exposed urban streets, or air-conditioned
 transport—takes up to 25 minutes indoors (7), but remains unclear outdoors.

            1.3. Aims, hypotheses, research questions, contributions to knowledge development (approx. 5 lines)

 This study explores commuter comfort via walking, cycling, and tram through different urban
 environments. Hypothesis: 1) Participants will show improved physiological and
 psychological well-being in green vs. impervious areas. 2) Acclimatization time will be
 estimated for green and impervious spaces. 3) Physiological and psychological
 responses will align. 4) Noise & activity will amplify thermal discomfort.

            1.4. Description of the population of interest and of the concrete procedures used, in a way that makes
                 it clear what is expected of the participants (approx. 20 lines)
                                                                                                 Commission d’Ethique IPSY – page 5 / 9
                                                                      Information aux membres de la Commission, version 1 mars 2022

1. Population: This study will recruit 30 participants (aged 18–40) with equal gender
representation (50% male, 50% female), determined via power analysis (in Appendix A).

2. Experiment: Participants will engage in three phases: 1) a green environment, 2) an
impervious urban environment, and 3) a tram journey. In the green and impervious phases,
both walking and biking (tricycle) will be performed for 30 minutes each; during the tram
phase, participants will remain seated for 30 minutes. In each phase there will be a 10-minute
break for participants to rest, drinking water will be provided. Duration of experiment 1:30–
5:30 pm in Etterbeek, with tram used to transition between phase 1 & 2.

3. Participants will wear four non-invasive sensing devices: 1) Empatica Embrace Plus
smartwatch (measuring EDA, PPG, wrist skin temperature), 2) Pupil Labs Neon smart
glasses (eye-tracking), 3) LYS Button (measuring illuminance) and 4) Atmotube PRO
measuring air pollution. Sensors 3 & 4 will be worn on participant’s shirt or jacket.

4. Environmental conditions will be measured by a researcher carrying a uCM backpack
system (thermal stress, temperature, humidity, radiation, wind, sound, GPS, comfort indices)
(8). If any clarification is required for environmental sensors, head of ethics commission of
LAB – Prof. Sergio Altomonte has proposed to get in touch with him.

5. Participants will complete shortened psychological & comfort-related questionnaires
(STAI-6 (10), PRS-11 (9,11), CSV, TSV (13)) & two custom questions before & after each
exposure. Breaks are scheduled between sessions for recovery, survey completion, and
sensor checks. Participation is voluntary, with a right to pause or withdraw at any time.

