6. Data confidentiality

Regarding data confidentiality, a distinction must be made between personal archives used for research purposes
(videos, tests/retests, etc.) and information made public (courses, scientific communication).

a) Does the information stored allow the participant to be identified (e.g. name, date of birth, voice, photo,

video image, etc.)? Yes      No

     If yes, which information and for what purpose?

b) What procedures are in place to ensure the anonymity of participants and the confidentiality of data
     during processing and dissemination (e.g. coded transcription, data encryption or digitalisation, erasure
     of tapes, aggregated data, omission of certain characteristics, use of a pseudonym, destruction of

    questionnaires, etc.)? The data collected will be pseudonymized. Each participant will be
    assigned a code (initials of the experimenter - control group/experimental group -
    participant number) (e.g. PP - ctrl - 001 or PP - expe - 002). Each participant will be
    recorded as code and participants will never be identified by their first name or in any
    other recognizable way, in any lists, results or publications related to the study. These
    data will be stored on the principal investigator and co-investigators' personal
    computers, where only the persons concerned will have access to the data through a
    secured common cloud storage server operated by IT department to minimize the risk
    of unauthorized acces (e.g. Microsoft OneDrive and Uclouvain private server). All
    computers used by the project team will be password encrypted. The protection of
    personal data is ensured by the law of July 30, 2018, on the protection of privacy and
    by European (European General Regulation on the Protection of Personal Data
    [RGPD] of May 25, 2018) and Belgian regulations in force. According to the RGPD,
    participants have a right to inspect the processing of their data. If they have any
    questions in this regard, they may contact the promoter or UCLouvain's data
    protection officer, as explained in the consent letter. If they have a complaint about
    the way their data is processed, they can contact the Belgian Data Protection
    Authority: Rue de la Presse 35 - 1000 Brussels - Tel: 02 274 48 00 - e-mail:
