                                             Recruitment Poster

                                   Figure 2: Poster – Participation Call
Call to be shared for the experimental campaign:
We are recruiting 30 individuals aged 18-40 for a research study examining comfort, stress,
and perception during walking and biking. The study aims to explore whether individuals feel
greater discomfort in impervious areas, whether green spaces reduce stress and enhance
emotional well-being, and how different modes of movement (such as biking versus walking)
may vary in perceived effort or stress.
Participation involves performing walking, biking and riding trams in Brussels in green and
urban spaces. You'll wear non-invasive and non-intrusive sensors measuring heart rate, skin
conductance, and temperature, along with eye-tracking glasses and button for measuring
illuminance due to sunlight, while completing brief surveys about your emotional state. All
instruments are safe, your data will remain confidential, and you may pause or withdraw at
any time. If interested, please contact us here: pranav.pandya@uclouvain.be.
                                               Annex 5
                                           Debriefing Participants

Thank you for taking part in the experiment “Assessing urban mobility comfort - commuting
across distinct urban environments.”

What We’re Hoping to Learn:
 i) You may have felt more discomfort in areas without greenery, especially when it was

     hot.
 ii) Green spaces may help reduce stress and improve how people feel.
 iii) Urban noise may have felt more discomforting with in thermal stress.
 iv) We’re also checking if what people report feeling matches their body’s signals (like heart

     rate or sweating).

All the information you provided will remain confidential and will only be used for research
purposes. You maintain the right to withdraw your data within 30 days by contacting us. If
you have any questions about this research, please reach us out at
pranav.pandya@uclouvain.be
                                       Appendix A
               Sample Size Calculation for the Experimental Campaign

Working Hypotheses

    1- Participants will show improved physiological and psychological well-being in green
         vs. impervious areas.

    2- Acclimatization takes longer after green and air-conditioned settings due to greater heat
         stress contrasts.

    3- Physiological and psychological responses will align.
    4- Noise & activity will amplify thermal discomfort.

Software Libraries
    1. NumPy: Numeric arrays and basic calculations
    2. SciPy: Statistical tests and curve fitting
    3. Statsmodels: Power analysis routines (TTestPower, FTestAnovaPower)
    4. Pingouin: Repeated-measures ANOVA power (power_rm_anova)

Statistical Methodology
Three a priori power analyses align with the primary within-subject design:

    1. Paired t-test for the diurnal contrast (Green vs Impervious).
    2. Repeated-Measures ANOVA across mobility modes (walk, bike, tram), each

         performed in afternoon.
    3. Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test for comparing subjective and physiological responses

         (e.g. reported vs. measured comfort).

Key Statistical Parameters

    • Significance level (α): 0.1
    • Target power (1 – β): 0.80
    • Within-subject conditions: 5 (walk-green, bike-green, tram, walk-urban, bike-urban)
    • Effect-size grid (expecting medium to large effect size):

             o Paired t: Cohen’s d = 0.50 (Medium), 0.80 (Large)
    • Cohen’s f notation (medium = 0.25, large = 0.40)
    • Dropout cushion: +5% recruitment

