Conclusion

This study will recruit 30 participants. This sample size of 30 was obtained based on the
following statistical calculations (with the assumption that medium effect size is achieved):

    1. Paired t-tests are used to detect mean differences between two conditions measured
         within the same participant. In this experiment, they are applied to compare
         psychological and physiological outcomes between green and impervious urban
         environments (Hypothesis 1), as well as between biking and walking. Based on a
         medium effect size (d = 0.50), this results in a sample size requirement of 27
         participants.

    2. Repeated measures ANOVA can detect differences across several repeated conditions
         within participants. In this experiment, each participant is observed under five mobility–
         environment combinations (walking, biking, and tram, across green and impervious
         settings), allowing assessment of interaction effects such as acclimatization and thermal
         comfort dynamics (Hypothesis 2 & 4). Considering a medium effect size (f = 0.25), a
         required sample size of 17 participants was obtained.

    3. Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test is applied to assess the association between subjective
         thermal comfort responses and physiological sensor data, such as heart rate, EDA, or
         skin temperature (Hypothesis 3). With a medium effect size (d = 0.50), the sample size
         requirement is 28 participants.

Based on the more conservative requirement of 28 participants and accounting for a 5%
potential attrition rate, the study will recruit 30 participants in total. This within-subject design
allows each participant to experience all conditions, minimizing individual variability and
fatigue, while enabling the collection of continuous real-world data and ensuring sufficient
statistical power to test the primary hypotheses. Hypothesis 5, which examines the potential
amplifying effect of urban noise and activity on perceived thermal discomfort, is considered
exploratory and was not included in priori power calculations. It will be examined descriptively
using environmental data (e.g., sound pressure levels), but no formal statistical testing is
planned.
