                                              Discussion

Correlations between subsets of four and six items of the STAI were greater than
r = .90. Whether either is an acceptable short-form of the STAI will depend upon the
extent to which they are reliable and valid in comparison with the full-form of the
STAI.

                      STUDY 2: RELIABILITY AND VALIDITY

The aim of this study is to determine the reliability and validity of the four- and six-
item short-forms of the STAI as a basis for choosing the optimal short-form. Internal
reliability was assessed. Test-retest reliability was not calculated for, as Spielberger
notes, test-retest reliability is low for state anxiety because of state anxiety’s
transitory nature (Spielberger, 1983). The form of validity for a short-form of the
STAI to be assessed is concurrent validity, i.e. the extent to which scores on the
short-form are similar to those obtained using the full-form. This is assessed for
groups with mean STAI ratings near the population mean (unselected pregnant
women), as well as groups with known raised anxiety (pregnant women following
receipt of an abnormal test result during pregnancy).

          Method

Subjects

The subjects comprised three groups: Group 1: 38 medical students, attending a psychology lecture;
Group 2 : 45 student nurses, attending a nursing lecture ; Group 3 : 200 pregnant women, as in Study
1 , above ;and Group 4: 23 pregnant women who had received an abnormal result on a routine screening
test for fetal abnormality. Data from these women had been obtained as part of another study (Marteau,
Cook, Kidd, Michie, Johnston, Slack & Shaw, in press).

Procedure

Questionnaires were completed while subjects waited for lectures (Groups 1 and 2), awaited a routine
clinic appointment (Group 3), and at home, using a postal questionnaire (Group 4).

                                                 Results

The reliability coefficient for the 20-item STAI was: 01 = .91; for the six items:

a = 3 2 ; for the four items: a = .77.

The comparability of prorated scores from the six- and four-item scales with the use
of the full scale was used to determine the concurrent validity of the two short-forms
of the STAI. To avoid over-inflated correlations caused by items correlating with
themselves (Pierce, Sarason & Sarason, 1986), the six-item questionnaire was
compared with both the 20- and 14-item questionnaires, and the four-item
questionnaire was compared with both the 16- and 20-item questionnaires.
Comparisons were made using paired t tests (see Table 2).

   There were no differences in the mean scores obtained using the full-form of the
STAI and those obtained using the prorated six-item short-form, for any of the three
