groups. Similarly, there were no differences between the means prorated from the six-
item short-form and the means prorated from the remaining 14 items. The prorated
means obtained from the four item short-form were all significantly higher than the
means based on the 16 remaining items and the full 20 items. Given this evidence
suggesting the validity of the six-item short-form, further analyses were conducted
with just this short-form of the STAI.

   T o determine whether the six-item short-form of the STAI is sufficiently sensitive
to detect the changes in anxiety detected by the full-form, STAI scores obtained
using the six-item short-form were compared with those obtained using the full-form
on a sample with known elevated anxiety. Following receipt of an abnormal result
on maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein screening indicating increased risk of spina
bifida and Down’s syndrome, women’s anxiety scores are significantly raised
compared both with their levels prior to testing, and when compared with women
receiving a normal test result (Marteau e t a/., in press). The mean STAI score for
these women immediately following receipt of an abnormal test result is 46.4
(SD 14.8); the mean score prorated from the six-item short-form is 47.7 (SD 15.8):
(22) = 0 . 9 6 , ~= .35). Thus, the mean score obtained from the six-item short-form
is similar to that obtained using the full-form of the STAI.

                                        General discussion

The six-item short-form of the STAI (STAI-6: Appendix A) produces scores similar
to those obtained using the full 20-item STAI. It has acceptable reliability, and
produces scores that are similar to those produced with the full-form across subject
groups manifesting normal and raised levels of anxiety. Two of the three anxiety-
absent items are those identified by Spielberger to be particularly sensitive to low
Six-item short-form of state scale of STAI  305                                                                 20448260, 1992, 3, Downloaded from https://bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.2044-8260.1992.tb00997.x, Wiley Online Library on [16/04/2025]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License

stressors ; all three anxiety-present items he reports as being particularly sensitive to
high stressors (Spielberger, 1983). When compared with the full-form of the STAI,
the six-item short-form offers a briefer and more acceptable scale for subjects; it
maintains results that are comparable to those obtained using the full-form of the
STAI and remains sensitive to different degrees of anxiety.

   There are several advantages to using a brief questionnaire. It is likely to maximize
response rates, and minimize the number of response errors and unanswered items,
thus improving the validity and generalizability of any findings. The longer a
questionnaire, the more the responding sample will be biased in favour of those who
find reading and writing easy. A brief questionnaire will also reduce the time taken
to complete the questionnaire, and the time taken to score the results.

